{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f4cbfc58a683894a66b028420969bb75a35fe4acda1bd3dce0024062c11b2041", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "\"To access the print version of the news article, a link is provided. The rules for solving the Sudoku puzzle are explained, with a notable exception to the standard rules. This exception involves cells contained within grey lines, which have a unique requirement. The numbers in these cells must add up to the figures indicated in the corner. This adds a new layer of complexity to the puzzle.\n\n\n \nAddition to the standard Sudoku rules includes the condition that no number can be repeated within each shape formed by grey lines. This means that each shape must contain a unique set of numbers, adding to the challenge of solving the puzzle. The rules are designed to test problem-solving skills and logic. Solving the puzzle requires careful attention to detail and a systematic approach.\n\n\n \nThe Sudoku puzzle is a popular activity for many people, providing a fun and challenging way to exercise the brain. The puzzle is typically solved using logic and reasoning, rather than relying on guesswork. By following the rules and using a systematic approach, solvers can work their way through the puzzle and eventually arrive at the solution. The sense of accomplishment upon completing the puzzle is rewarding.\n\n\n \nFor those who are unable to solve the puzzle on their own, a solution is available. The completed puzzle can be found in next week's Observer Digital Edition. This provides a way for solvers to check their work and see the correct solution. The digital edition is a convenient way to access the solution, and it can be easily viewed on a variety of devices.\n\n\n \nThe Observer Digital Edition is a valuable resource for puzzle enthusiasts. It provides access to a wide range of puzzles, including the Sudoku puzzle. The digital edition is easy to use and navigate, mak